description:
Found by an external review and re-measured by architect-bb193f43 at main 0c215e82 on 2026-09-11.

THE REPRODUCTION: with real SQLite and production handlers, installing a NEWER policy that declares NO auto-approval opt-ins still allowed preview approval to proceed under an OLDER opted-in policy. An operator who installs a policy to turn automatic approval OFF does not turn it off.

MEASURED, in `apps/daemon/src/preview/preview-auto-composition.ts`, `evaluationSliceOf`:

    const installed = installedSlices(stateOf(readDurableLedger(store, projectId), policyAggregateId(projectId)));
    const refs = Object.keys(installed).filter((ref) => {
      const slice = installed[ref];
      return slice !== undefined && sliceKindOf(ref, slice) === "EVALUATION" && declaresPreviewOptIn(slice);
    }).sort();
    const ref = refs.length === 1 ? refs[0] : undefined;

This filters the ENTIRE historical installed set down to EVALUATION slices that declare a preview opt-in, then requires exactly one survivor. Installing a newer, opt-in-free policy does not remove the older opted-in slice from that set, so exactly one still survives and it still governs. Automatic RELEASE, by contrast, selects the NEWEST evaluation slice. Two gates, two different rules for the same question.

Note the function's own header already argues that requiring a single declaring slice is the fail-closed reading, because two declaring slices would make the winner depend on key order. That reasoning is sound for AMBIGUITY and does not address SUPERSESSION. Read the whole header before changing it; the fix is not simply to take the newest.

THE DECISION THIS ROW OWES. One explicit rule for which policy is effective, applied by BOTH gates, that also answers how an operator disables automatic approval. That is a product decision, not only a code change - if the plan cannot settle it from the existing contracts, report blocked and route it rather than guessing.

VERIFY BEFORE PLANNING: read `preview-auto-composition.ts` in full and find the release-side selector; do not plan against the excerpt above.
